      "worked": "The original domain is $D=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R:cx+d\\ne0\\}$, with $(c,d)\\ne(0,0)$ so that the expression defines a function on a nonempty domain. Since $f$ is even, $x\\in D$ also gives $-x\\in D$. Thus both $cx+d$ and $-cx+d$ are nonzero for every $x\\in D$.\n\nFor every $x\\in D$,\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-x)&=f(x)\\\\\\frac{-ax+b}{-cx+d}&=\\frac{ax+b}{cx+d}\\\\(-ax+b)(cx+d)&=(ax+b)(-cx+d)\\\\-acx^2+(bc-ad)x+bd&=-acx^2+(ad-bc)x+bd.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis identity holds on the infinite set $D$. Comparing coefficients of $x$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}bc-ad&=ad-bc\\\\2ad&=2bc\\\\ad&=bc.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThis proves the necessary condition. The condition alone does not guarantee evenness: the original domain must also be symmetric about $0$.",
